---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Arrow from '../components/Arrow.astro';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  active="About"
  description="Christopher Hynes’s path through statistics, economics, empirical research, and computer science."
>
  <section class="page-intro shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">About / Christopher Hynes</p>
    <h1>
      Curiosity across fields.
      <br />
      <em>Rigor across methods.</em>
    </h1>
    <p class="page-deck">
      I like questions that call for both careful analysis
      <br class="desktop-break" /> and something concrete built.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="about-grid shell">
    <div class="about-label">
      <span class="monogram large-monogram" aria-hidden="true">
        ch.
      </span>
      <p class="eyebrow">
        Researcher mindset.
        <br />
        Builder instincts.
      </p>
      <a class="text-link" href="/assets/resume.pdf">
        My résumé <Arrow diagonal />
      </a>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>A connected path</h2>
      <p>
        I’m an M.S. candidate in Computer Science at Georgia Tech, with a B.S. in Statistics and
        Economics from the University of South Carolina Honors College. My work spans empirical
        research, machine learning, data analysis, and software development.
      </p>
      <p>
        Economics taught me to ask what a comparison can actually establish. Statistics gave me
        tools for reasoning about uncertainty. Computer science lets me turn an idea into an
        experiment, an interface, or a working system.
      </p>
      <p>
        As an undergraduate research assistant, I worked with economic, demographic, and
        public-health data on questions involving STEM-worker agglomeration, spatial segregation,
        and racial disparities in birth outcomes. At GreyNoise, I applied analysis to customer
        retention, lead conversion, and finance workflows.
      </p>
      <h2>How I approach the work</h2>
      <p>
        I start with the question and the decision it might inform. Then I look for a credible
        comparison, an implementation I can inspect, and a way to communicate what the result
        does—and does not—support.
      </p>
      <p>
        That approach connects projects that otherwise look quite different: a synthetic-control
        thesis, a replication of a neural architecture, an interface study, and local LLM
        applications. I’m interested in research and applied work where those connections are
        useful.
      </p>
      <h2>Beyond the desk</h2>
      <p>
        I play guitar, backpack, travel, and study languages. I’ve visited 32 countries and speak
        advanced Spanish, intermediate German, and basic French. A year studying in Madrid was part
        of that path.
      </p>
      <figure class="travel-figure">
        <img
          src="/assets/been.jpeg"
          alt="A personal map showing countries I have visited."
          width="3600"
          height="1950"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>A different kind of fieldwork: places I’ve visited.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <a class="text-link" href="mailto:resume@chynes.org">
        Start a conversation <Arrow diagonal />
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
